<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">North Africa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> is the region of Africa along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, bordered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> to the south. It was the home of the Punic and Garamantian states and later a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-muslim-conquests">Arab conquests</a> it was ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun</a> and Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphates</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> empires such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a>, and it was linked to West African states by trans-Saharan trade.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/north-africa.jpg" alt="North Africa" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Connormah · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:North_Africa_(orthographic_projection).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
